      "id": 1,
      "text": "Mill City was founded in the late 19th century when several mills were built along the North Santiam River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitoregon.com/cities/mill-oregon/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Mill City takes its name from various lumber mills, including the Hammond Lumber Company and Santiam Lumber Company.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mill_City%2C_Oregon"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "One of the oldest landmarks in Mill City is a former railroad bridge that now serves pedestrians and cyclists.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mill_City%2C_Oregon"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Mill City Enterprise is the longest-running newspaper in town and is currently being archived by the University of Oregon.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mill_City%2C_Oregon"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Mill City's economy remains rooted in its history as a lumber town, with several mills still in operation today.",
